From: Radeon24 <Radeon24@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 23 Oct 2026 20:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] usb: gadget: net2280: optionally move the NET228x data by PIO

On the FortiGate 100D (NET2282 on a conventional PCI bus, legacy INTx
shared with the EHCI and UHCI controllers), a sustained host-to-device
transfer on a CDC NCM gadget hits the WARN_ON() of start_dma(): after
a short OUT packet, handle_ep_small() writes DMA_ABORT and waits 50 us
for DMA_ENABLE to clear, ignores the timeout, and restarts the queue
while the engine still reports enabled. The OUT endpoint then stops
completing requests and the host side times out its transmit queue.

Bring back the use_dma module parameter (removed in 2014, when the DMA
was deemed reliable), default on, NET2280/NET2282 only: with use_dma=0
endpoints ep-a to ep-d use the PIO paths the driver still has for
ep-e/ep-f, so that the DMA engines can be ruled in or out on such a
board. Nothing changes with the default value.

Assisted-by: LLM
Signed-off-by: Radeon24 <Radeon24@users.noreply.github.com>
---
--- a/drivers/usb/gadget/udc/net2280.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/gadget/udc/net2280.c
@@ -142,6 +142,18 @@
 /* "modprobe net2280 enable_suspend=1" etc */
 module_param(enable_suspend, bool, 0444);
 
+/* use_dma -- When disabled, endpoints ep-{a,b,c,d} of the NET2280 and
+ * NET2282 move their data by PIO, one interrupt per packet, instead of
+ * through their DMA engines: slower, with more CPU load, but it keeps
+ * the DMA engines out of the way on a board where they misbehave.
+ * The USB338x always uses DMA.
+ */
+static bool use_dma = true;
+
+/* "modprobe net2280 use_dma=0" */
+module_param(use_dma, bool, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(use_dma, "NET2280/NET2282: use the DMA engines of ep-a to ep-d (default: yes)");
+
 #define	DIR_STRING(bAddress) (((bAddress) & USB_DIR_IN) ? "in" : "out")
 
 static char *type_string(u8 bmAttributes)
@@ -533,7 +545,8 @@
 	/* synch memory views with the device */
 	(void)readl(&ep->cfg->ep_cfg);
 
-	if (!ep->dma && ep->num >= 1 && ep->num <= 4)
+	if (!ep->dma && ep->num >= 1 && ep->num <= 4 &&
+	    (use_dma || (ep->dev->quirks & PLX_PCIE)))
 		ep->dma = &ep->dev->dma[ep->num - 1];
 
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&ep->dev->lock, flags);
@@ -2163,7 +2176,7 @@
 
 		if (tmp > 0 && tmp <= 4) {
 			ep->fifo_size = 1024;
-			ep->dma = &dev->dma[tmp - 1];
+			ep->dma = use_dma ? &dev->dma[tmp - 1] : NULL;
 		} else
 			ep->fifo_size = 64;
 		ep->regs = &dev->epregs[tmp];
